INT. TROLL BRIDGE TOLLHOUSE - DUSK

A stone hut under a mossy arch. A kettle on a hook. Coins in neat stacks. Rain taps the slate roof.

BRUNHILDE GRUMBLEDOWN, 100s, a troll in a patched apron, stirs the kettle. Her knuckles are mossy, her hands slow. Her son, ULFRIC, 40s, sits in a corner with his huge back to her, whittling a bone flute that will never be finished. A lean elf, ERLANDEL, 20s, slips in from the rain and shakes out his cloak; he slides three silver coins onto the counter without a word. A small goblin girl, SPRIGGET, 8, perches on the windowsill and swings her feet.

ERLANDEL
Evening, Mother Brunhilde.

BRUNHILDE
Erlandel. You're thinner.

ERLANDEL
The road is long.

BRUNHILDE
So is the winter.

Ulfric does not turn. The flute scrapes. Sprigget leans toward Erlandel and whispers, loudly.

SPRIGGET
He hasn't spoken since the flood.

ULFRIC
(low, to the wall)
I speak.

BRUNHILDE
Ulfric.

ULFRIC
I speak when there's something worth saying.

Brunhilde ladles tea into four cups and sets them on the counter, one at a time, nudging the last toward her son. He reaches back without looking and takes it. Erlandel drinks. Sprigget hops down, steals a coin, and slips it into her sleeve while everyone pretends to watch the kettle. Rain pours.
